Alloy: winemac.drv: scroll like a Windows mouse wheel while a game holds the cursor.

In games, a small flick of the wheel did several wheel steps, resting a finger on a Magic Mouse or trackpad
scrolled, a swipe kept scrolling after the finger lifted, and scrolling made the camera jump. Many games (Unreal
Engine ones, for example) treat every WM_MOUSEWHEEL as one step, whatever its size.

On Windows a wheel click is one WHEEL_DELTA (120) message and never moves the cursor. On the Mac:
- macOS smooths each wheel click into a burst of up to about 20 scroll events, all with the click's timestamp and no
  gesture phase, and Wine sent each one as a wheel message;
- every small movement of a finger on a trackpad or Magic Mouse, and every momentum (inertia) event after a swipe,
  became a wheel message;
- each one was sent with MOUSEEVENTF_ABSOLUTE | MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE to where the Mac cursor is. In mouse-look the Mac
  cursor isn't where the game keeps the Windows one, so that move turned the camera.

While a game holds the cursor (it is clipped with ClipCursor or the game hid it), the driver now:
- sends one WHEEL_DELTA per wheel click, counting each burst once;
- for trackpad and Magic Mouse gestures, sends one WHEEL_DELTA per 20 points of swipe, keeps the remainder for the
  next event, and starts over when a touch begins or ends or the direction reverses, so a touch alone does nothing;
- ignores momentum events, as Windows has no scroll inertia;
- sends the wheel without moving the cursor, and keeps the next mouse move relative.
Other windows (launchers, Steam, apps) keep Wine's smooth, precise scrolling.

